  "worked_solution": "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{1}{x}=\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\operatorname{cot}\u2061x=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x})}{\\operatorname{cot}\u2061x} & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{{[\\frac{1}{x}]}^{\u2032}}{{[\\operatorname{cot}\u2061x]}^{\u2032}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{-{csc}^{2}\u2061x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}\u2061x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{{sin}^{2}\u2061x}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={({lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x}{x})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x})}{\\operatorname{cot}\u2061x} & ={({lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x}{x})}^{2} \\\\  & ={({lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{[\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x]^{\u2032}}{[x]^{\u2032}})}^{2} \\\\  & ={({lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x}{1})}^{2} \\\\  & ={(\\operatorname{cos}\u20610)}^{2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
